The display registers of the replaced sceGs* handlers follow the game's own SDK code when a host GS is installed

What it fixes: with a real GS (src/gsbridge, patches 0004-0009, and the host program of T-110) the picture
was four times too narrow, because the runtime's replaced sceGs* handlers wrote their own idea of the
display set-up instead of what the game's own SDK code writes (docs/knowledge/18-display-registers.md
sections 3, 4 and 6): sceGsResetGraph sent a stand-in A+D packet through the GIF whose entries 0x41 and
0x42 a real GS reads as SCISSOR_2 and ALPHA_1 (overwriting the game's values; the host program carried a
stop-gap filter, filter_reset_graph, to keep them away from the GS), SMODE1, SRFSH, SYNCH1, SYNCH2 and
SYNCV were never written at all, and DISPLAY was built with MAGH 0 (a quarter of the horizontal size).
That stand-in packet was also what wrote PMODE, DISPFB and DISPLAY with the runtime's values.

What it does, and only with a host GS installed (PS2Runtime::setHostGs; without one the runtime's own GS
keeps every legacy value, pinned by the test):
  * sceGsResetGraph(0): no GIF packet; CSR reset (0x200), the CSR revision byte into the gparam block,
    IMR = 0xFF00, then the SetGsCrt syscall - exactly what the game's own sceGsResetGraph does
    (knowledge/18 section 2).
  * GsSetCrt: with a host GS and NTSC interlace (video mode 2) it writes the BIOS's measured video
    registers SMODE1 0x740814504, SMODE2 0x1 (frame) or 0x3 (field), SRFSH 0x8, SYNCH1 0x7F5B61F06F040,
    SYNCH2 0x33A4D8, SYNCV 0xC7800601A01801 (knowledge/18 section 1). Any other mode keeps the legacy
    registers and logs one line.
  * sceGsSetDefDispEnv, sceGsSetDefDBuff, sceGsSetDefDBuffDc and sceGszbufaddr build what the SDK's own
    functions build: the PMODE 0x66 / SMODE2 / DISPFB / DISPLAY formulas of section 2, MAGH =
    (w + 0x9FF) / w, DX/DY from the NTSC/PAL mode tables, DH = h-1 (frame) or 2h-1 (field), DTHE for a
    16-bit psm, the A+D tag of 8 (14 with the clear packets) registers, the second buffer's frame page at
    half the z-buffer page, and the z-buffer page count of the frame. sceGsPutDispEnv -> applyGsDispEnv
    puts the display environment on circuit 2 only (revision != 1), as the SDK does.
  * No output mode other than NTSC (omode 2) and PAL (omode 3) has a formula here; those keep the legacy
    values and log one line each. Everything the runtime's own renderer needs is untouched: gsHostMode()
    is false without a host GS.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/host_gs_display_test.cpp (new) fails against the tree without this
patch (11 checks: no SMODE1/SRFSH/SYNCH1/SYNCH2/SYNCV, DISPLAY2 with MAGH 0, PMODE 0x8007, IMR 0, and the
stand-in packet's 0x41/0x42/0x59..0x5F entries among the 9 GIF packets the display set-up forwards) and
passes with it (the six video registers equal to knowledge/18 section 1, DISPLAY2 = the SDK's
0x1BF9FF0183227C = the table's DISPLAY1, DISPFB2 = 0x1400 = the table's DISPFB1, no stand-in entry and
exactly the 8 draw-environment register writes forwarded, and the legacy path unchanged when no host GS
is installed). tests/host: `uv run --no-project --with pillow --with pytest python -I -m pytest tests/host
-q` -> 8 passed, with the synthetic guest setting the display up only through these handlers and with
filter_reset_graph and DON_HOST_GS_FILTER removed from src/host/gs_glue.cpp. With the game, a 60 s run
writes a 640-pixel-wide screenshot and its registers at the memory-card dialog are compared with the
console's own dump; the numbers are in the report of T-108.

Origin: original work for this project (T-108). The SDK behaviour the handlers now follow is measured and
written down in docs/knowledge/18-display-registers.md (decompiled from the game's own copy of the SDK).
